faq:
  - q: "How do you write a counter offer letter?"
    a: "Four elements: (1) Open with genuine appreciation — not performative, but real enthusiasm for the role. (2) State your counter clearly — a specific number, not a range. (3) Anchor to market data and your scope — not to personal expenses or what you currently earn. (4) Signal that you want to make it work — you are negotiating, not threatening. Keep it under 200 words. Shorter is stronger."
  - q: "Should you always counter a job offer?"
    a: "Almost always yes. Employers build negotiation room into initial offers. A professional counter — 10–20% above the offer, anchored to market data — almost never loses an offer. Employers expect it. The risk of not countering is leaving money on the table permanently. The risk of a professional counter is almost zero."
  - q: "How much should you counter a job offer?"
    a: "10–20% above the initial offer is the standard range for a first counter. This gives the employer room to meet you partway while still moving you meaningfully above the initial offer. If your research shows you are more than 20% below market, you can anchor higher — but explain it clearly with market data."
  - q: "What if you counter and they say no?"
    a: "You have several options: (1) Accept the original offer if it still meets your needs. (2) Counter on non-base components (signing bonus, equity, title, review timeline). (3) Ask what would need to change for them to revisit compensation. (4) Decline if the gap is too large and you have other options. In almost no case does a professional counter result in an offer being rescinded — that is an extremely rare outcome with a reasonable ask."
  - q: "Do you need to give a reason for your counter offer?"
    a: "Briefly, yes — anchor to market data and scope. 'Based on my research on the market for this role and level' is sufficient. You do not need to explain personal expenses, cost of living increases, or what you currently earn. The anchor should be external (market data) not internal (your needs)."
